<h1>power</h1>
<p>This will return the value of a number multiplied by itself &quot;n&quot; number of times. For example, <span class="inline">power(5,3)</span> will multiply 5 by itself 3 times and return 125, which is the same as saying 5*5*5=125. Please note that the &quot;x&quot; value
(the number to change) <i>cannot</i> be a negative value.</p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Syntax:</h4>
<p class="code">power(x, n);</p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th>Argument</th><th>Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>x</td><td><span data-keyref="Type_Real"><a target="_blank" href="../../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">Real</a></span></td>
<td>The number to change.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>n</td><td><span data-keyref="Type_Real"><a target="_blank" href="../../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">Real</a></span></td>
<td>How many times to multiply x by itself.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p> </p>
<h4>Returns:</h4>
<p class="code"><span data-keyref="Type_Real"><a target="_blank" href="../../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">Real</a></span></p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p class="code">score += power(dmg, 3);</p>
<p>This will add the value of held in the variable &quot;dmg&quot; to the power of 3 to the score variable.</p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
